1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,152
Coding agents can already write plausible bug fixes.

2
00:00:04,402 --> 00:00:07,479
But nobody ships one without a human reading it first.

3
00:00:07,929 --> 00:00:13,052
Notin-Loop is a dark factory for code fixes: an always-on SRE agent.

4
00:00:13,302 --> 00:00:17,935
It watches a live server and runs every incident through deterministic gates.

5
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:22,394
Demo one: we break the cart endpoint, and empty carts start crashing.

6
00:00:22,644 --> 00:00:27,512
It investigates, reproduces, fixes, inspects the diff and runs a canary.

7
00:00:27,762 --> 00:00:31,408
Every gate passes, so it ships, with nobody touching anything.

8
00:00:31,858 --> 00:00:35,673
Demo two: a cache race. The replay fails one time in three.

9
00:00:35,923 --> 00:00:40,221
The evidence is unsure, so the agent refuses to guess and escalates.

10
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:45,419
Demo three: a fix passes the tests and the canary, but breaks a rare report.

11
00:00:45,669 --> 00:00:49,643
The watch window catches it, and the agent rolls back its own release.

12
00:00:50,093 --> 00:00:55,192
Only an unbroken chain of passes changes the server. Anything else stops the line.

13
00:00:55,442 --> 00:00:59,034
Every decision goes into a signed, hash-chained audit log.

14
00:00:59,484 --> 00:01:01,856
Notin-Loop. Humans on the loop, not in it.
